00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:40.020
Is that right?

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:40.890
BJORN LINDBERG:
Yeah, pretty much.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:41.310
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.710
BJORN LINDBERG: I used to say my
job is, like get players online

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.800
and get them to play with
other players if they want to.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.700
Or they can play
themselves if you want to.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.110
That's your choice,
basically, as a player.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.735
But you shouldn't be like a big
hurdle going online and finding

00:04:51.735 --> 00:04:52.530
a friend.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.965
You don't want to run through
enormous amounts matchmaking

00:04:54.965 --> 00:04:56.340
lobbies just to
play five minutes

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.775
and then sit in matchmaking
lobbies for another 20 minutes

00:04:58.775 --> 00:05:00.900
to get to play five minutes
and that kind of stuff.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.760
You just want it to be
smooth, generally speaking.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:03.840
You want to go press
a button, say, I

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.730
want to play with other players
while I'm doing something.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:07.770
And eventually we find you
some players to play with.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.187
And you say, yes,
and then you get

00:05:09.187 --> 00:05:11.510
teleported to where you want
to be, and there you go.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.050
MARK MANDEL: No, that
makes a lot of sense.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:14.730
ROBERT MARTIN: So you
mentioned it's a single-player

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:16.110
or a multi-player game.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.210
How important would you
say for a game like this

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.392
the multi-player aspect is?

00:05:19.392 --> 00:05:21.850
BJORN LINDBERG: The way you
should think about it, I think,

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:22.350
is this.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.222
Most players approach
games like, oh, I

00:05:24.222 --> 00:05:25.180
can play a team player.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:27.157
I can play it myself, right?

00:05:27.157 --> 00:05:29.490
At some point, they're going
to say, well, this was fun,

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.740
but maybe it's fun if I play
with my friends, for instance.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.270
And then you can
form a co-op group

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.700
and play together against
the environment, so to speak,

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.320
and all that AI whatever, right?

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.847
You don't want that stuff
to be too large for players

00:05:39.847 --> 00:05:41.430
to build the group
with their friends.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.303
Eventually someone's
going to say, oh, well,

00:05:43.303 --> 00:05:44.970
there's none of my
friends online today.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.390
I'm going to play
with random people.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.150
I want a pick up
group, like match-make,

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:50.750
to get into group with players.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.725
Same thing there, you don't want
it to be a large step forward.

00:05:53.725 --> 00:05:55.350
And eventually you
end up in this sense

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:58.260
where it's like some players
are going to go to PVP gaming,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.090
where they play
against other players.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.830
And in a way, that's
the perpetual game,

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.020
where it just goes on forever
and ever because the content is

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.130
other players, in a way.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.900
That sounds weird,
but it's really--

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.040
at some point, you're
going to run out

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.260
of things to do essentially.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.010
So you want to play
against other players.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.718
We know from experience
that not everyone

00:06:12.718 --> 00:06:14.670
is going to end up in
PVP because it's like,

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:16.212
me, for instance,
I never go there.

00:06:16.212 --> 00:06:17.670
I don't like to
fight other people.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:18.798
I'm a teamwork guy.

00:06:18.798 --> 00:06:20.340
I want to play with
other players not

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:21.460
against other players.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:22.627
So it's kind of your choice.

00:06:22.627 --> 00:06:25.740
But we don't want it to force
you into that thing, right?

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.292
So the importance here
is that it's available.

00:06:28.292 --> 00:06:30.750
But you can still enjoy the
game as a single-player player,

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.140
if you would want to.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:35.800
And there's no hard line
that you need to, like,

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.370
go press a bunch of buttons
in a UI and get there,

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.100
and it's complicated long
waiting times and that stuff.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.485
MARK MANDEL: I hear the
launch went really well.

00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:44.250
But can you give us
a sense of the scale

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.750
of the game or how many
players or anything

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:46.840
along those sort of lines?

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:48.030
BJORN LINDBERG: The
problem with online games

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:49.710
is that you've
released your game,

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.190
and then everyone comes at
that launch pretty much, right?

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.890
And when I say at
launch, I mean usually

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.180
during a couple of weeks.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:58.410
It takes a couple
of days for people

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.330
to pick it up in all the
regions around the world

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:01.070
and stuff like that.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:02.737
But usually the first
and second weekend

00:07:02.737 --> 00:07:04.510
is going to be a
whole lot of players.

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:07.790
And if you compare this to,
say, I make my own website,

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:10.590
I invite my friends to
test my new photo sharing,

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.290
whatever it is, right, then
maybe 10 guys in the beginning,

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:14.873
and you figure out
a bunch of the bugs

00:07:14.873 --> 00:07:16.050
and then word of mouth.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.682
And two years in, you have
your 10 million daily users

00:07:18.682 --> 00:07:19.640
or something like that.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.690
You have fixed most of the
technical problems that

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:22.815
could happen to that point.

00:07:22.815 --> 00:07:23.760
We can do scaling.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:25.410
It's not that big of a deal.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.090
We have kind of
the problem, where

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.190
we need to do scale they want.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.600
And we don't really
have a choice.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.260
So to scale here,
it's coming down to

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.420
basically having, A, all
the computability resources,

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:37.920
so to speak.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.930
Like, you have all the machines
that you would ever need

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.130
and also making sure that
everything works together.

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:43.135
Because even if you
have all the machines,

00:07:43.135 --> 00:07:45.640
it doesn't necessarily mean
all the intercommunication that

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.710
happens in the back end is
just available or working.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:48.040
So

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.750
A lot of testing,
obviously, leads up

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:50.890
to this point where
you say, OK, let's

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:53.170
test this thing specifically
and see what it does.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:54.970
And then you try to
separate as much as possible

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:56.470
in the code base
so you don't end up

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:58.450
in cases where
things are weirdly

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:00.460
inter-depending on each
other in a way where

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:02.170
weird stuff happens, basically.

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:04.120
Because testing a
whole system with all

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.020
these machines is very hard.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:08.785
But simulating one million
real players, for instance,

00:08:08.785 --> 00:08:10.410
is not something easy
to do because you

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:12.400
need to have all these
connections coming

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:13.570
from somewhere, et cetera.

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:14.560
MARK MANDEL: There's
no service where

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.916
you can just ask a million
of your closest friends?

00:08:16.916 --> 00:08:17.910
BJORN LINDBERG: No.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.790
Maybe we can certainly
[INAUDIBLE] a million

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:20.290
employees.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:22.790
[INAUDIBLE] game
and say, go play.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:23.930
That's not the case.

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:24.730
ROBERT MARTIN: So
the scale challenge

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:26.170
really comes in at
launch because there's

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:28.295
a bit of an unknown with
how many players are going

00:08:28.295 --> 00:08:30.460
to show up on that
first day and where

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:31.030
it's going to go from there?

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:32.679
BJORN LINDBERG: Well, we
have a reasonable clue.

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:35.090
But still the system as a
whole is complex, right?

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:37.679
It's simple parts that make
complex behaviors together.

00:08:37.679 --> 00:08:39.870
And we can test individual
parts and say, well,

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:41.370
I'm going to assume
that we're going

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.570
to see x amount of
requests per second

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:44.750
on this particular service.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.250
And I'll test it for 10 times
as much and see that it works.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.282
That I can do it
relatively easily.

00:08:49.282 --> 00:08:50.740
But it doesn't mean
that it doesn't

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.300
have some weird
global side effect

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:53.950
in some other part of the
system that you didn't

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:55.150
think about or something.

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:56.410
It can be configuration
errors that

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.640
are hard to track down because
there's just so much of it.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:08:59.210
ROBERT MARTIN: So
that's interesting.

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:00.910
You mentioned the
different components.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:02.618
What are some of the
different components

00:09:02.618 --> 00:09:04.870
that it takes to run a
multi-player game like this

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:05.560
on the cloud?

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.893
BJORN LINDBERG: I mean, we're
microservice architecture.

00:09:07.893 --> 00:09:09.860
It's the easiest way to
get around this hurdle.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:11.740
You want to have a
divide-and-conquer strategy

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.420
and say, OK, you guys,
you do matchmaking.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:14.440
You guys, you do plans.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.280
You guys, you do log-in, et
cetera, that kind of stuff,

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:16.780
right?

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:18.792
And [INAUDIBLE]
services basically we

00:09:18.792 --> 00:09:19.750
have a common platform.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:21.535
Everything works, so obviously,
because otherwise there

00:09:21.535 --> 00:09:23.785
would be too much different
technologies in the space.

00:09:23.785 --> 00:09:26.163
But supposedly we have
a bunch of services.

00:09:26.163 --> 00:09:28.330
And some of them are essential
to get into the game.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:29.530
Like, you need to
be able to log in.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:31.738
You need to be able to find
a game server, et cetera.

00:09:31.738 --> 00:09:33.940
Those things are tested
to death basically.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:35.483
You want that flow to work.

00:09:35.483 --> 00:09:37.150
However, if you have
some other service,

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:38.935
like, I don't know,
say, leaderboards,

00:09:38.935 --> 00:09:40.480
and it has a hiccup
or five minutes,

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:41.440
it's not the end of the world.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.540
And then you can focus resources
on things that are essential.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:44.810
And then other things--

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.373
if the leaderboard
service crashes,

00:09:46.373 --> 00:09:48.290
it's not going to tear
down the entire system.

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:50.165
That's the benefit
of microservices.

00:09:50.165 --> 00:09:51.790
But it's also an
organizational benefit

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.650
because these guys can work
on their own, so to speak.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.410
You don't have to
synchronize everything

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:57.972
with everyone else
that works online.

00:09:57.972 --> 00:10:00.055
MARK MANDEL: Now, you also
mentioned game servers?

00:10:00.055 --> 00:10:00.420
BJORN LINDBERG: Yeah.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:01.610
MARK MANDEL: What
does that control?

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:02.580
BJORN LINDBERG: So what
I'm doing generally

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:04.030
is getting people
to a game server.

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:05.110
That's my goal, basically.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:06.970
Get them as smooth as
possible to a games server.

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:08.440
And once we get them
to a game server,

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:09.490
it's a whole different area.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:10.690
We have a bunch of
teams, obviously.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.273
But organizational
wise, in this case,

00:10:12.273 --> 00:10:14.680
we are the online back-end
team that does everything

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.560
by orchestration and having
all the service available,

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:20.660
et cetera, working with our
infra guys that basically fix

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.650
the hardware, the virtual
hardware from Google,

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:24.350
to basically make sure
that's up and running.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:25.810
But then we have
the gameplay team.

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:27.920
And they do the game
client and the game server.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.607
So in the game server,
we do portions of it.

00:10:30.607 --> 00:10:32.440
Like, we provide network
stack and libraries

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:33.290
for that kind of stuff.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:34.748
But they do all
the gameplay logic.

00:10:34.748 --> 00:10:37.095
And then if you compare
the relative complexity

00:10:37.095 --> 00:10:38.470
of these two
things, like, if you

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:41.050
take all the online back end and
compare it to the games server,

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:42.400
the game server's
still more complicated

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:43.990
because it can do
so many things.

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:45.760
You have all these messages
for different things

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:46.385
you want to do.

00:10:46.385 --> 00:10:48.220
I want to fix something
in my inventory.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:49.488
I want to press the button.

00:10:49.488 --> 00:10:50.530
I want to shoot that guy.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:51.340
I want to move here.

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:52.180
I want to do all these things.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.013
And to share interaction
of all these things

00:10:54.013 --> 00:10:56.980
is going to make it very hard
to test properly and have

00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:00.017
100% coverage on every single
case of everything, obviously.

00:11:00.017 --> 00:11:01.600
So maybe I should
answer your question

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:03.642
of what the game server
does is basically running

00:11:03.642 --> 00:11:04.990
the game simulation for you.

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:07.660
And the client will send these
orders to the game server.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:09.320
They're picked up
on a frame basis.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:10.780
So the game server
runs on frames,

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:13.210
process all that stuff for
your output, output should be,

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.110
and send it back to the player
and then do the next frame.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:16.640
And it runs like that forever.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:18.932
ROBERT MARTIN: So if I'm
playing a game of "Division 2"

00:11:18.932 --> 00:11:21.790
with a few of my friends, we
all connect to a game server.

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:23.885
That's our simulated world
that we share gather.

00:11:24.032 --> 00:11:24.865
BJORN LINDBERG: Yep.

00:11:24.865 --> 00:11:28.260
It runs AI and knows what the
general layout of the world

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:29.970
is to a certain fidelity.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:31.350
Like, it knows, here's a wall.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:33.430
It maybe doesn't know there's
a piece of trash on the ground,

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:35.862
but it knows that here's a
wall, and here's a bunch of AI

00:11:35.862 --> 00:11:36.820
and that kind of stuff.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:37.990
ROBERT MARTIN: So each
time you get a new group

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:40.380
of players that shows up
and wants to play the game,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:41.870
we need an additional
game server.

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:42.070
BJORN LINDBERG: No.

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:43.100
It holds them together.

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:44.172
So for various reasons--

00:11:44.172 --> 00:11:46.380
the first one is that it's
a resource problem, right?

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.110
You don't want a new
process every single time

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.735
because you need to boot it, and
it eats such a bunch of memory

00:11:50.735 --> 00:11:51.790
just by loading, right?

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.290
You need to load in an entire
world to have all this static.

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:57.100
It's called static, dimension
static, like physics.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:58.450
It houses whatever, right?

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:02.150
And that's quite expensive
because the game is essentially

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:05.300
a piece of Washington, DC,
quite large piece, by the way.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:07.190
So you need to load
all that into memory.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:08.982
But then you're adding
one player for that.

00:12:08.982 --> 00:12:11.137
It's like 1 megabyte worth
of memory for a player.

00:12:11.137 --> 00:12:13.720
But you load it up 10 gigabytes
of RAM just to host the world.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:15.160
So you probably want to
add a bunch of players

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:16.330
to make it break even, kind of.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:18.550
So we host something, between
depending on hardware,

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:21.058
we are like 800 to 1,000
players per server.

00:12:21.058 --> 00:12:22.600
But for most part,
you don't see them

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:25.203
because you instantiate
copies of the world.

00:12:25.203 --> 00:12:27.370
So when you go online,
you're going to see yourself,

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:28.020
and that's it.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:28.880
If you watch your
friend, you're going

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:31.450
to put you into the same
instance of the world

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:32.200
as you have.

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:33.590
MARK MANDEL: So you
kind of multi-tenant it.

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:35.340
BJORN LINDBERG: The
gaming term is phases,

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:36.640
but it's kind of multi-tenant.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.015
MARK MANDEL: So it sounds like
running those game servers

00:12:39.015 --> 00:12:40.540
at scale is a tricky problem.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.260
How do you do that
on Google Cloud?

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:43.490
BJORN LINDBERG: Yeah,
we're basically having

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:44.910
a bunch of compute instances.

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:46.970
So the way it kind of
work is that the client

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:49.490
is going to figure out which
data center it would prefer.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:51.357
But when it goes
online, it's going

00:12:51.357 --> 00:12:52.440
to ping every data center.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:53.400
Which one is closest to me?

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:54.050
OK, this one.

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:56.060
I would like, I don't
know, Europe somewhere.

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:58.052
And in that data
center, we have a bunch

00:12:58.052 --> 00:12:59.510
of game servers
running, basically.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:03.110
And various computing centers
runs in binary, and that's it.

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:04.270
It's very simple.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:06.110
The reason for that
is because if we

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.672
were to do this today, carte
blanche, nothing prior,

00:13:08.672 --> 00:13:10.130
we would probably
go, I don't know.

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:11.700
Here's a bunch of interesting
products from the Google

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:12.620
catalog we could use.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:15.140
However, we have been doing
this for 20 years or so.

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:16.520
And we have a
bunch of technology

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:18.650
that solves a bunch of
these problems already.

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:20.580
It's easier just to
re-employ it and say, OK.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:21.050
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:22.460
And I guess, then,
once you want to scale,

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.502
it's a question of just
adding more GCE instances

00:13:24.502 --> 00:13:25.190
as it goes up.

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:25.820
BJORN LINDBERG: Yep.

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:27.445
ROBERT MARTIN: So
the launch is usually

00:13:27.445 --> 00:13:29.210
the trickiest part of a game.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:30.390
Can you walk us through it?

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:33.020
And I believe that "Tom Clancy
Division 2" is your first sort

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:34.250
of Google Cloud launch.

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:34.470
BJORN LINDBERG: That's true.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.380
ROBERT MARTIN: Can you walk
us through the experience

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:37.910
of launching a
game on the cloud?

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:39.550
You mentioned
different data centers.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.758
Maybe give us an idea of
the scale of the deployment.

00:13:41.758 --> 00:13:43.467
BJORN LINDBERG:
Deployment, I think we're

00:13:43.467 --> 00:13:45.243
in nine different regions.

00:13:45.243 --> 00:13:46.910
I think at launch we
were a little more.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:48.678
I think we were about 11 or 12.

00:13:48.678 --> 00:13:49.386
I don't remember.

00:13:49.471 --> 00:13:50.370
MARK MANDEL: Oh, interesting.

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:51.200
BJORN LINDBERG: Some
of these regions

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:53.277
are basically just
more data capacity

00:13:53.277 --> 00:13:55.610
because you guys want us to
scale between different data

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:56.110
center.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:58.130
But they're both in,
like, American West Coast.

00:13:58.130 --> 00:13:59.780
And there's literally
no difference

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:02.330
from a player's point of
view at this point, Later on,

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:03.680
you can scale down a
little bit because you

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:06.060
don't have the same concurrency
as you had when you launched.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:07.020
Yeah, it's quite large.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.530
But we try to automate almost
everything because there's

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:10.570
no one that's going to
sit there and say, start

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:11.737
another server and all that.

00:14:11.737 --> 00:14:14.750
It usually happens 9:00
in the evening in Sweden

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:16.265
and then goes on until midnight.

00:14:16.265 --> 00:14:17.390
And people want to be home.

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:18.590
So we want to automate.

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:21.380
And then you add the tons
of alarms for various things

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:22.170
that could happen.

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:23.753
And then you'd best
make sure that you

00:14:23.753 --> 00:14:25.405
can fix it if it happens.

00:14:25.405 --> 00:14:27.530
I think your question was,
if it was smooth or not?

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:27.770
Or--

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.630
ROBERT MARTIN: Yeah, just
interested in the experience

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:30.905
of launching a
game in the cloud.

00:14:30.905 --> 00:14:31.870
BJORN LINDBERG: Yeah,
so my experience is it

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:33.950
was very smooth because we had
done it on physical hardware

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:34.340
before.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:36.050
And generally speaking,
that's a little more work

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:37.590
because you need to
provision the hardware,

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:39.080
and you need to get to the
install, yada yada, all

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:40.280
those kinds of things
you need to do,

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:42.770
and you have someone there
running down in data center

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:44.853
and changing [INAUDIBLE]
or whatever it is, right?

00:14:44.853 --> 00:14:46.850
And this time we learned APIs.

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:48.860
We run everything through
Salt and Terraform.

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:50.150
And it's up and running.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.850
And we auto scale a bunch
of stuff into the cloud

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:54.100
as well, which you can do now.

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:55.340
So it's very convenient.

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:58.642
And you don't necessarily
bother too much about it.

00:14:58.642 --> 00:15:00.350
I'm not the who's guy
sitting there doing

00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:02.480
all the work in that
sense because I'm not

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:04.100
doing deployment so much.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:06.430
But I got the impression
from the people doing it

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:09.200
that this is way easier,
less work for us,

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:11.600
and you can trust it to
actually work much more

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:12.640
than you could before.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.015
It kind of makes
sense, in a way,

00:15:14.015 --> 00:15:16.430
because you deploy a generic
interface, so to speak.

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:18.850
You don't care too much about
what we put them on and vice

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:19.430
versa, right?

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:21.620
So we don't talk too much about
what you're doing on your end.

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:23.330
From that perspective,
yeah, I think

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:25.730
it's a sensible approach to
do it like that because it's

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:26.938
easier for everyone involved.

00:15:26.938 --> 00:15:29.230
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it's
actually really interesting you

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:31.230
mentioned that you
started in 11 regions,

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:32.817
and then you sort
of scaled down.

00:15:32.817 --> 00:15:34.900
Is that just because you
know your traffic better?

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.608
Is that why, sort of
like a cost savings?

00:15:36.608 --> 00:15:38.025
BJORN LINDBERG:
Yeah, pretty much.

00:15:38.025 --> 00:15:39.500
And in some cases,
like when we say

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.210
regions, what we
really mean is, like,

00:15:41.210 --> 00:15:42.700
we don't mean South America.

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:44.210
We mean data centers.

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:46.850
And in some cases, as I said,
some of these data centers

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:48.710
are basically equivalent
of each other,

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:50.218
like their next
door geographically.

00:15:50.218 --> 00:15:51.635
And from a player's
point of view,

00:15:51.635 --> 00:15:53.630
it doesn't really matter if
you're on that one or that one.

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:55.190
They are both good, so to speak.

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:58.160
And the reason we spread out is
because you don't necessarily

00:15:58.160 --> 00:15:59.780
know the capacity,
what's it going

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:02.300
to look like in 2 hours
or 15 hours or whatever

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:04.915
and say, OK, let's just be
everywhere, just to be safe.

00:16:04.915 --> 00:16:07.290
And we don't know if you're
going to run out of resources

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:08.457
in a particular data center.

00:16:08.457 --> 00:16:10.880
So it makes sense to pre-warm
one and have it ready to go,

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:11.840
if you need to.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:13.490
MARK MANDEL: Actually, for
those who may not be aware,

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:14.510
can you talk a
little bit about why

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:15.710
you have to be in
so many regions

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:17.510
and what the latency
requirements are there?

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:18.560
BJORN LINDBERG: Game
servers, so what

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:21.060
happens is you run them at a
certain framework, in our case,

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:23.180
about 30 times a second,
20 to 40 times a second,

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:24.500
depending on what you're doing.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:26.800
And you want to get that
message to the game server

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:27.980
within a reasonable time.

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:29.810
If it takes like
200 milliseconds

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:33.320
to get there and then the server
processing time, plus the trip

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:36.050
back, you add a half a second
behind all the time, right?

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:38.330
From when I press something,
it takes half a second

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.753
before my client can
acknowledge the effect, right?

00:16:40.753 --> 00:16:42.170
If you have too
much latency, it's

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:43.740
going to be noticeable
at some point.

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:45.650
In particular, say, I'm
living in Australia.

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:46.770
And I have to connect to Europe.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.190
It's going to be not incredibly
interesting to play that game.

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:51.525
So you want to be close to
the player for that reason.

00:16:51.525 --> 00:16:53.150
Well, generally, what
we do, of course,

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:55.120
we hide a lot of this latency.

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:58.330
As an example, we can have
a guy, I'm in my scope.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:01.240
Tiny scopes I have a couple
of degrees field of view.

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:02.780
And a guy runs
through the scope,

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:05.180
and I want to shoot them in
the head as he runs by me.

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:08.000
I'm going to allow my client to
say you shot him in the head,

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.310
even though we don't know
for a fact that's the case

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:11.099
until later.

00:17:11.099 --> 00:17:13.690
And then if that was the case,
and let's pretend everything's

00:17:13.690 --> 00:17:14.190
fine.

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:16.520
If that wasn't the case, we just
remove the effect, so to speak.

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:18.349
Oh, you didn't lose
that health actually.

00:17:18.349 --> 00:17:20.043
Because otherwise it
becomes too laggy.

00:17:20.043 --> 00:17:21.710
So you need to apply
effect immediately,

00:17:21.710 --> 00:17:22.603
ask if it happened.

00:17:22.603 --> 00:17:24.020
And then you need
to correct later

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:25.880
if the game server
disagrees for some reason.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:28.088
And you have a bunch of
techniques in the game server

00:17:28.088 --> 00:17:29.930
to make sure that you
can figure this out.

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:32.150
Because when the game
server gets the message,

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:33.480
it's like 100 milliseconds ago.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:34.938
So it needs to go
back and see what

00:17:34.938 --> 00:17:36.500
happened 100 milliseconds ago.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:37.712
Where were everyone?

00:17:37.712 --> 00:17:38.670
Oh, that guy was there.

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:40.810
OK, you did that head
shot or you didn't.

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:41.590
MARK MANDEL: And you
say you also have

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:42.860
a variety of microservices.

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:45.250
Do you also spread those
around in a variety of regions?

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:45.940
Or do you have them centrally?

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:47.830
BJORN LINDBERG: We spread them
over one region in Europe.

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:49.997
So that's mostly what we
call the core system, which

00:17:49.997 --> 00:17:52.660
is, like, what you log into,
where you find your friends,

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:55.370
where you create a clan,
where you fiddle around

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:57.120
with your which character
should I choose?

00:17:57.120 --> 00:17:58.995
What game server should
a play on, et cetera,

00:17:58.995 --> 00:18:00.100
those kind of things.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:02.200
And it's not particularly
latency sensitive.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.600
It doesn't really matter
so much if it takes

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:06.070
like half a second to log in.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:07.072
You're going to do it
once, and then you're

00:18:07.072 --> 00:18:08.155
going to play for an hour.

00:18:08.155 --> 00:18:08.800
No one cares.

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:09.880
ROBERT MARTIN: So
these core services

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:11.288
can be sort of
centrally located.

00:18:11.288 --> 00:18:13.038
It's really the game
servers that you need

00:18:13.038 --> 00:18:13.530
to get closer to the players.

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:14.110
BJORN LINDBERG:
Yeah, those the ones

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:15.550
that we need to
spread out, yeah.

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:16.770
ROBERT MARTIN: Yeah,
that's interesting.

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:18.728
And so you mentioned
you're using nine regions.

00:18:18.728 --> 00:18:20.590
We have about 20 regions.

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:22.390
What's sort of the
balance in finding

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:25.780
the right number of regions and
versus the number of players

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:28.720
that you can find to
play within those?

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:30.970
BJORN LINDBERG: I mean, for
finding the right regions,

00:18:30.970 --> 00:18:32.320
you want to be
everywhere, basically.

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:34.060
You want to be in South
America and the United States.

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:35.320
And you want to be
in Africa, et cetera,

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:36.140
et cetera, et cetera, right?

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:36.970
So you want to be everywhere.

00:18:36.970 --> 00:18:38.370
In many places,
there's not going

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:41.020
to be a whole lot of players
during particular hours.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:42.850
Like, if you do
downtime in Australia,

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:44.950
it's not a whole lot of
players playing, right?

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:46.630
Autoscaling makes it
easier, obviously.

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:49.193
So finding the right
number of services

00:18:49.193 --> 00:18:50.860
is not the problem
that we want to solve

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:53.410
on a daily basis, where we would
add like a stand up and say,

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:55.310
yesterday we ran out of
capacity in Australia.

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:56.560
We should probably add
one more game server.

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:57.768
No, we don't want to do that.

00:18:57.768 --> 00:19:01.425
It's, like, we can automate
that relatively straightforward.

00:19:01.425 --> 00:19:02.175
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:19:02.175 --> 00:19:04.660
And did you have a strong
relationship with Google Cloud

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:05.535
when you set this up?

00:19:05.535 --> 00:19:06.910
Did we help you out in any way?

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:08.200
BJORN LINDBERG: We have
Marcus [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:09.620
And I brought in a
bunch of tech people.

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:10.750
So we're sitting around there.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.130
And we're playing a game
and waiting for something

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:12.730
to go bad.

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:14.220
I don't think anything
really went bad.

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:15.000
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that's nice.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:16.720
BJORN LINDBERG: Marcus is
shaking his head like, nope,

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:17.428
nothing went bad.

00:19:17.428 --> 00:19:19.338
So you have everyone there.

00:19:19.338 --> 00:19:21.130
So we have this big
war room upstairs where

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:22.255
we have all these monitors.

00:19:22.255 --> 00:19:25.655
And everyone is represented, so
to speak, all the stakeholders.

00:19:25.655 --> 00:19:27.530
So we can figure out if
something goes wrong,

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:28.963
you can figure
out what and where

00:19:28.963 --> 00:19:30.130
and who should deal with it.

00:19:30.130 --> 00:19:31.422
And usually you go to everyone.

00:19:31.422 --> 00:19:33.940
It's like, we have this problem,
you can you see anything?

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:35.080
Can you go look at
your monitoring?

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:36.580
And say, can you
see anything weird?

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:37.350
Like, nope, OK.

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:38.180
We check around.

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:39.830
Ah, here's something, OK.

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:40.330
Dig dig.

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:42.100
ROBERT MARTIN: It sounds like
the launch went really well.

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:44.380
But this was your first
sort of cloud-native launch

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:45.162
with Google Cloud.

00:19:45.162 --> 00:19:46.870
What were some of the
challenges that you

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:48.245
encountered in
launching the game

00:19:48.245 --> 00:19:49.510
and how did you overcome them?

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:51.177
BJORN LINDBERG: From
a cloud perspective

00:19:51.177 --> 00:19:53.770
in particular, or generally,
and from a cloud perspective,

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:56.470
there wasn't something that was
really worrying, so to speak.

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:59.030
The way we see hardware is
that OK, here's a machine.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:02.030
We write everything for what you
would call commodity hardware,

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:03.610
like garden-variety services.

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:06.280
Like, we don't have a
requirement of special machines

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:08.470
with very expensive
database machines

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:10.600
with tons and tons of
memory, anything like that.

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:12.100
So as long as you
provide hardware,

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:13.430
we can usually run on it.

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:14.930
Game servers are a
little different.

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:17.388
They need to be quite large
mostly because you want to cram

00:20:17.388 --> 00:20:19.120
in those 5,000-ish
players on them.

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:20.980
No not really,
there's nothing that

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:22.630
was particularly complicated.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:25.090
There's always this little
bug you need to go find.

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:28.300
But usually in my software,
like most of the things

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:29.327
just work right.

00:20:29.327 --> 00:20:31.410
MARK MANDEL: That just
sounds too good to be true.

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:32.440
BJORN LINDBERG:
Yeah, it is, kind of.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:33.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:34.300
MARK MANDEL: I mean,
I'm not complaining

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:34.880
from the Google side.

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:36.010
BJORN LINDBERG: No, no, no.

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:38.390
I know it always sounds like
it's too good to be true.

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:42.215
But we did for "Division 1,"
and a lot of the technology

00:20:42.215 --> 00:20:43.840
is the same, obviously,
because there's

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:46.480
no reason to go around and
reinvent it from scratch again,

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:46.700
right?

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:47.920
We just say, OK,
here's everything

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:48.920
we did for "Division 1."

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:51.190
Of course we make changes,
particularly like things,

00:20:51.190 --> 00:20:52.570
like, we added leaderboards.

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:54.580
We added cloud,
new microservices.

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:57.010
But the model and how you
structure it around it

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:57.910
is the same.

00:20:57.910 --> 00:20:58.810
We are microservices.

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:00.640
Here's the team doing
that feature, basically.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:02.760
MARK MANDEL: But "Division 1"
wasn't run on Google Cloud.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:03.190
BJORN LINDBERG: No.

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:03.670
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:04.900
When you came over
to Google Cloud,

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:07.000
were there particular things
you were looking to improve on

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:09.450
as you came across that you
found that were possible now

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:10.700
that you were on Google Cloud?

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:11.770
BJORN LINDBERG:
Yeah, autoscaling,

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:13.180
automatic deployment
of services,

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:15.550
that's like number one, I
think, and also ease of it.

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.050
It's much easier
for us to just say,

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:19.100
here's the script that
runs Terraform and sets up

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:19.767
the environment.

00:21:19.767 --> 00:21:21.005
It's like, boom, done.

00:21:21.005 --> 00:21:22.630
It saves a lot of
time also when you're

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:25.695
doing testing because you have
all the first-party experts,

00:21:25.695 --> 00:21:26.570
Microsoft, et cetera.

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:27.930
And they want to certify
the build obviously.

00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:29.263
So they need a particular build.

00:21:29.263 --> 00:21:30.820
And it needs to be
online, of course.

00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:31.778
So you can set up them.

00:21:31.778 --> 00:21:34.390
Like, here is your system that
runs this particular build,

00:21:34.390 --> 00:21:36.880
slang for worse and so to
speak, in case someone wonders.

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:38.920
But here's your
particular build.

00:21:38.920 --> 00:21:40.548
Do all your testing
as you please.

00:21:40.548 --> 00:21:42.340
And we're going to
update it in seven days.

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:44.090
So we get the licensing
rights and whatever.

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:46.060
And they have a certain
stint where they do testing.

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:47.393
And same thing for localisation.

00:21:47.393 --> 00:21:49.270
You have a bunch of guys
going in and saying,

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:50.770
is this is the
Spanish localization?

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:52.007
Great, basically.

00:21:52.007 --> 00:21:53.590
They need a copy of
everything running

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:54.673
with a particular version.

00:21:54.673 --> 00:21:57.400
And you might end up in QA,
Quality Assurance, et cetera,

00:21:57.400 --> 00:21:58.570
that does all the
same things, right?

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:01.112
And you want them to be all be
online in the same environment

00:22:01.112 --> 00:22:02.140
as live would be like.

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:05.290
And for a day, of course,
you're all on different builds

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:07.272
because why would we
have been on the same?

00:22:07.272 --> 00:22:08.230
That would be too easy.

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:09.772
But you can [INAUDIBLE]
environments.

00:22:09.772 --> 00:22:11.810
So it saves a lot of time
because when you do it

00:22:11.810 --> 00:22:13.810
on fixed hardware, someone's
going to say, well,

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:14.600
this is expensive.

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:15.350
Can you have less?

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:16.050
OK, sure.

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:17.590
You can run the game
live on less hardware

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:18.340
and cut, cut, cut,

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:19.450
And then we end
up, oh my god, we

00:22:19.450 --> 00:22:21.492
need to also have all
these testing environments.

00:22:21.492 --> 00:22:22.750
Where do we put them anyway?

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:25.792
We want them to be the same as
much as possible, obviously,

00:22:25.792 --> 00:22:27.250
because that's what
you're testing.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:29.470
At least you want all the
configuration of everything

00:22:29.470 --> 00:22:31.060
to be relatively the same.

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:32.950
So the difference between
a test environment

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:34.390
and the live
environment, in our case,

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:36.040
is generally that we
don't allow people

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:38.373
in unless they have certain
properties on their account.

00:22:38.373 --> 00:22:40.420
And we basically
whitelist IP ranges

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:42.270
and say, these are
allowed to connect,

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:43.520
these studios or these people.

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:44.437
That's the difference.

00:22:44.437 --> 00:22:46.000
Otherwise, it's
pretty much the same.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:47.708
ROBERT MARTIN: It
sounds like the ability

00:22:47.708 --> 00:22:49.740
to spin up environments
when you need them,

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:51.590
experiment with
different regions

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:54.378
were really kind of keys
to the success this time.

00:22:54.378 --> 00:22:55.920
BJORN LINDBERG:
Yeah, I would say so.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:57.340
You get to a point
where you basically say,

00:22:57.340 --> 00:22:59.380
I want to test this as
live-like as possible.

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:00.120
And that's it.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:02.540
Being able to spin
them up as you want

00:23:02.540 --> 00:23:04.212
saves a lot of
time for everyone.

00:23:04.212 --> 00:23:05.170
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:05.730
BJORN LINDBERG:
Our core business

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:06.630
is not running servers.

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:08.460
Our core business
businesses to make games.

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:11.370
And in a way, we want to
get away as much as possible

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:12.773
from the nitty-gritty details.

00:23:12.773 --> 00:23:14.190
On the other hand,
we have a bunch

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:15.840
of people who are super
interested in these things.

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:17.445
So we like to try
to strike a balance.

00:23:17.445 --> 00:23:19.103
It becomes interesting
for everyone.

00:23:19.103 --> 00:23:20.520
MARK MANDEL: I'm
curious if you're

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:22.580
going to do this all over again,
is there anything that you

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:23.968
would change or do differently?

00:23:23.968 --> 00:23:25.260
BJORN LINDBERG: No, not really.

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:26.360
Not from the GCP side.

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:28.450
We look at it from
what is the game?

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:30.125
What is the player experience?

00:23:30.125 --> 00:23:31.500
If the player
experience dictates

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.890
we need to change something,
then we do change something.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:36.522
And as I said earlier, we're
running on commodity hardware.

00:23:36.522 --> 00:23:38.730
So it's unlikely that we're
going to say, oh, my god,

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:40.560
we need completely
different hardware.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:42.450
It's like, it's just
still the same Intel

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:44.950
machines in the bottom running
on a virtualization layer.

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:46.658
And it's like, maybe
we change the specs,

00:23:46.658 --> 00:23:49.038
like less cores, more cores,
less memory, et cetera,

00:23:49.038 --> 00:23:49.830
that kind of thing.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:52.210
But to me, that's a slider
in the UI on a web page

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:52.710
pretty much.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:54.540
And that's kind of
what I like about it

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:57.167
because you don't have to make
these decisions very early.

00:23:57.167 --> 00:23:59.000
If you do them physically,
you need to go in

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:00.917
and say, OK, we're going
to this kind of game.

00:24:00.917 --> 00:24:02.640
What does it mean in
terms of hardware?

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:04.440
What is the hardware
profile we're looking for?

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:05.050
I don't have to.

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:06.900
It's like, yeah, how
many cores do you want?

00:24:06.900 --> 00:24:07.700
OK, I want 16.

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:08.220
OK, good.

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:08.890
There are 16.

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:09.402
Go.

00:24:09.402 --> 00:24:11.360
MARK MANDEL: Now, you
also mentioned previously

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:14.160
having dashboards and monitoring
and keeping this thing alive.

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:15.360
I mean, launch is one thing.

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:17.670
But keeping it alive over
a period of time, what do

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:20.003
look at there for a monitoring
of game servers and games

00:24:20.003 --> 00:24:21.128
services there?

00:24:21.128 --> 00:24:23.170
BJORN LINDBERG: So the
way it works kind of like,

00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:24.760
first you have your
monitoring layer,

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:26.520
which can tell us basic
stuff about the hardware,

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:27.780
to a level where
you're monitor it,

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:29.550
like CPU consumption and
bandwidth and that kind

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:30.842
of stuff, which is interesting.

00:24:30.842 --> 00:24:32.617
But in the code,
we basically have--

00:24:32.617 --> 00:24:33.700
it's called a data sample.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:36.033
It's essential a way to just
say, here's the data point,

00:24:36.033 --> 00:24:37.930
and that can end up
in various places.

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:40.920
But one of them is
InfluxDB in Grafana.

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:42.280
And then we have Zabbix.

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:44.970
So what we do in InfluxDB with
Grafana is draw all the graphs.

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:47.430
And we know roughly what
we want to look at and.

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:49.200
For Zabbix, it's most
like if this ever

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:52.140
happens or this value ever
is lower or higher than this,

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:53.370
you want an alarm.

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:55.810
And then you sort your
alarms according to severity.

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:58.350
And then some of them, it's
going to send you an SMS

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:00.360
and say, hey, you
should probably

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:02.670
look at this because this
shouldn't be like this.

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:04.150
It's a lot of them.

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:05.430
MARK MANDEL: It sounds like
he use a lot of open source

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:06.330
monitoring tools.

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:07.782
Did you choose
those for a reason?

00:25:07.782 --> 00:25:09.490
BJORN LINDBERG: We
choose them naturally.

00:25:09.490 --> 00:25:12.310
We have basically the same stack
for "Division 1," more or less,

00:25:12.310 --> 00:25:12.810
at least.

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:13.690
They're available.

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:15.260
There's certain problems you
don't want to bother with.

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:17.593
Like, you don't want to write
your own operating systems

00:25:17.593 --> 00:25:18.200
or databases.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:19.770
It just takes too much
time for a little game.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:21.780
You can use open source
stuff in this case

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:24.140
and just be on your merry
way doing games instead.

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.050
ROBERT MARTIN: And
are you monitoring

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:27.330
sort of technical elements
of the system operation?

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:30.030
Or is it more like player
experience type of metrics?

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:30.300
BJORN LINDBERG: We do both.

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:31.600
Player experience,
generally speaking,

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:33.510
it's a little bit harder
because you usually

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:34.752
look at aggregates of data.

00:25:34.752 --> 00:25:36.210
You need to say,
OK, I've collected

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:39.360
all this data about the
player experience so far.

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:41.860
Typical example is what
you can see, for instance--

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:44.700
and this is a classic example
of why in game analytics

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:46.770
everyone fails at level 7.

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:48.430
People stop playing at level 7.

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:49.045
Why is that?

00:25:49.045 --> 00:25:49.920
And then you go look.

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:51.900
Oh, this mission that you
have to play at level 7

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:52.983
is too hard, for instance.

00:25:52.983 --> 00:25:54.000
That's a typical thing.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.632
But you cannot see it as a
blip that happens just now.

00:25:56.632 --> 00:25:58.590
You need to look at the
entire history of the--

00:25:58.590 --> 00:25:59.790
ROBERT MARTIN: Trend over time.

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:00.780
BJORN LINDBERG: Yeah,
trends over time.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.215
And that's basically run on
a totally different system,

00:26:03.215 --> 00:26:04.590
because we are
solving two needs.

00:26:04.590 --> 00:26:08.250
One is I would like to
see what happens right now

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:12.270
Grafana dashboard, Big Graph
or Zabbix alerts telling me

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:13.020
what's happening.

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:15.690
Or we have even more
elaborate interfaces,

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:16.910
where I can basically go to--

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:19.410
Every server has to know the
web page where I can go and see

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:21.180
what do you think
about life right now?

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:22.620
Like, what's your
CP consumption?

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:24.310
How many file hands do
you have open, et cetera,

00:26:24.310 --> 00:26:25.200
that kind of stuff.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:27.850
While monitoring all of
the gaming experience

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:30.330
is usually something that you
put your data in a database.

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:31.320
You run an intel.

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:34.710
You get a report on whatever
cadence you want, basically,

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:36.270
as soon as you can
do it or weekly

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:38.730
or whatever, depending on what
area you're interested in.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:40.835
So generally, those take
a little longer time.

00:26:40.835 --> 00:26:41.110
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:43.060
And so that difference between
monitoring and analytics,

00:26:43.060 --> 00:26:43.420
right?

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:44.110
BJORN LINDBERG: Exactly.

00:26:44.110 --> 00:26:45.370
Generally speaking, you
can't act on it immediately

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.680
anyway because you need to do a
game, some change to the game,

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:47.880
right?

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:49.680
You need to make a new building,
a new change on the sign.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:52.080
You say, OK, that guy
was too hard to defeat.

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:54.040
I need to tune it
down a bit, right?

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:56.310
And you can't do that
with knob game design

00:26:56.310 --> 00:26:58.560
for all those ones have a
knob for every single thing.

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:00.400
But it's like to tune
it live, so to speak.

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:02.370
But it turns out to
be an impossibility

00:27:02.370 --> 00:27:04.148
because the number
of knobs basically.

00:27:04.148 --> 00:27:05.940
If it was, like, one
knob, you can probably

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.420
do it or 10 but not if we're
talking about literally

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:09.150
thousands.

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:12.205
I think thousands is low-ball
number for how many knobs

00:27:12.205 --> 00:27:14.310
they would want to have
ideally to tune everything

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:15.220
to their liking.

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:15.530
ROBERT MARTIN: Yep.

00:27:15.530 --> 00:27:17.535
So you'd need some time
to make adjustments.

00:27:17.535 --> 00:27:19.470
BJORN LINDBERG: Yeah, and
even if you have the knob,

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:21.120
you might not want to
touch it because you don't

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:22.090
know what's going to happen.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:23.370
You probably want to
run it through QA way,

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:25.090
right, because it's easier
to maneuver that thing

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:26.550
and say, oh, that's [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:28.505
And then, oh, my god,
now we have an exploit

00:27:28.505 --> 00:27:30.630
because a certain combination
of gear and whatever,

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:32.730
and you end up in a situation
where just people are fooling

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:34.050
that boss and get all the loot.

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:35.310
MARK MANDEL: And game
players will definitely

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:36.060
work that stuff out.

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:36.280
BJORN LINDBERG: Oh, yeah.

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:37.230
ROBERT MARTIN: Yes, they will.

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:37.920
BJORN LINDBERG: Absolutely.

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:39.600
I mean, you're testing
and testing and testing.

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:41.050
And the number of
hours of testing

00:27:41.050 --> 00:27:42.800
goes into product is staggering.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:44.620
It's like you wouldn't
imagine it, right?

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:45.520
And then you go live.

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:47.530
And then, like, two days
later, they find an exploit.

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:49.030
It's like, how did
you guys find it?

00:27:49.030 --> 00:27:49.940
OK, all you players.

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:50.830
You're gamers.

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:51.910
You're trained to that.

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:53.243
ROBERT MARTIN: It's the mindset.

00:27:53.243 --> 00:27:54.160
BJORN LINDBERG: Yeah.

00:27:54.160 --> 00:27:56.535
ROBERT MARTIN: So it's been
a great discussion about some

00:27:56.535 --> 00:27:58.180
of the services in
GCP that you use

00:27:58.180 --> 00:28:00.070
to launch "Division
2," Compute Engine

00:28:00.070 --> 00:28:03.850
and a few of our other sort of
global infrastructure services.

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:05.290
Maybe for the next
game, what else

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:07.780
could Google be doing to help
support your game launches?

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:10.018
What other types of
services and offerings

00:28:10.018 --> 00:28:11.560
do you think would
be most important?

00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:13.330
BJORN LINDBERG: I think from
our perspective, or at least

00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:14.760
my perspective,
as I said earlier,

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:16.145
we write a whole lot
of software ourselves.

00:28:16.145 --> 00:28:18.610
And we don't necessarily
depend so much on services

00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:19.578
to do things.

00:28:19.578 --> 00:28:21.370
The problem with service
generally speaking

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:22.570
is they usually involve
a machine [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:22.570 --> 00:28:23.370
to make a request.

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:24.622
And it's kind of
like it's a slow rate

00:28:24.622 --> 00:28:25.705
of response kind of thing.

00:28:25.705 --> 00:28:28.780
What I'm really interested in
is getting good open source

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:30.580
libraries to solve
common problems.

00:28:30.580 --> 00:28:32.590
Like, I would like
to have an SSL

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:35.860
library that is understandable,
have some reasonable source

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:36.465
code.

00:28:36.465 --> 00:28:38.680
And I don't need
[INAUDIBLE] 4 or Lambda 5.

00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:43.240
I need AAS and [INAUDIBLE]
and that modern stuff, right?

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:45.820
That doesn't necessarily
make too much assumption

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:48.065
about operating environment,
doesn't necessarily

00:28:48.065 --> 00:28:48.940
have to be Unix-like.

00:28:48.940 --> 00:28:51.700
We also need to build for
other platforms and just

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:53.130
Unix-like platforms.

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:56.262
And you have already open
sourced a bunch of compressors

00:28:56.262 --> 00:28:57.220
and that kind of stuff.

00:28:57.220 --> 00:28:59.110
I like that because it's
like it builds everywhere.

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:00.130
I can use it everywhere.

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:01.220
It's readily available.

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:03.520
It doesn't make a big
complicated installation.

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:06.340
You get a library file and a
header, and that's it, done.

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:06.840
Build it.

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:07.430
Done.

00:29:07.430 --> 00:29:09.513
And that kind of stuff
would be great to have more

00:29:09.513 --> 00:29:10.860
of because everyone wants it.

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:11.610
Everyone needs it.

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:14.340
It's just a convenience of
having available, so to speak.

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:16.507
ROBERT MARTIN: So really
kind of reusable components

00:29:16.507 --> 00:29:18.190
that lots of studios
and publishers

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:20.350
would all either have
to invent for themselves

00:29:20.350 --> 00:29:21.250
or go find somewhere.

00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:23.042
BJORN LINDBERG: We
don't have the resources

00:29:23.042 --> 00:29:25.480
to put a bunch of people on
building a new compression

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:28.253
library because we really don't
have that kind of resources.

00:29:28.253 --> 00:29:30.170
And same thing in crypto
is even worse, right?

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:32.180
Because even if you build your
own crypto and think it works,

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:34.150
it's going to be vetted for
10 years before [INAUDIBLE] it

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:34.760
basically.

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:36.700
And that kind of organization
is not available to us,

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:38.200
basically, because
we are relatively

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:40.010
small by comparison at least.

00:29:40.010 --> 00:29:42.422
And yeah, getting a
hold of that stuff, that

00:29:42.422 --> 00:29:44.755
would be great because you're
asking what services would

00:29:44.755 --> 00:29:45.040
we want to use?

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:46.660
I say, yeah, we
like your services.

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:48.778
But what we would like
even more is source code.

00:29:48.778 --> 00:29:50.570
MARK MANDEL: No, that
makes a lot of sense.

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:51.460
All right, well, fantastic.

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:53.050
Well, Bjorn, thank you
so much for spending time

00:29:53.050 --> 00:29:53.440
